 {
  "coord": "5:0",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "term",
  "note": "\"glass cabinets\" uses the banned word cabinet for the glass presses.",
  "replacement": "Late that afternoon Mr. Utterson called at Dr. Jekyll’s door, where Poole let him in at once. The butler took him through the kitchen quarters and over a yard that had once been a garden, to the building people called, without much distinction, the laboratory or the dissecting-rooms. The doctor had bought the house from the heirs of a famous surgeon. Since his own tastes were chemical rather than anatomical, he had put the block at the bottom of the garden to a different use. The lawyer had never before been admitted to this part of his friend’s premises. He studied the dingy, windowless building with curiosity, and as he crossed the theatre he felt an uneasy strangeness all around him. Eager students had once packed the place; now it lay gaunt and silent. The tables were loaded with chemical apparatus, crates stood about, packing straw littered the floor, and a dim light came down through the foggy dome. At the far end, a flight of stairs rose to a door covered in red baize, and through it Mr. Utterson was at last shown into the doctor’s study. It was a large room, lined with glass cupboards and furnished, among other things, with a full-length mirror and a business table. Three dusty, iron-barred windows looked out on the court. A fire was burning in the grate, and a lit lamp stood on the mantelpiece, since the fog was already thickening even inside the houses. Close up to the warmth sat Dr. Jekyll, looking deathly sick. He did not rise to greet his visitor. He held out a cold hand instead, and bade him welcome in a voice that had changed."
 },
